{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-180917",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=-26$ and ${a}_{5}=-12$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{3}}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12-(-26)}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+26}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{2} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=-26,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ -26 & ={a}_{1}+2(7) \\\\ -26 & ={a}_{1}+14 \\\\ -26-14 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-40+(n-1)(7) \\\\  & =7n-47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $17$th term by substituting $n=17$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{17} & =7(17)-47 \\\\  & =119-47 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
